Also, why were you doing this?

```
RegisterTool(toolType);
var registerMethod = typeof(MCPServer).GetMethod(nameof(RegisterTool))?.MakeGenericMethod(toolType);
registerMethod?.Invoke(this, null);
```

Isn't this redundant?

I made some changes to simplify the inner workings, for context I had originally intended this library to be a wrapper for an existing implementation, and there was/is still some tech debt from trying to squeeze it all together. in regards to the tool/function distinction, I have made the McpTool attribute now able to replace the function attribute, and it no longer is needed on the class. This will maintain backwards compatibility while aligning better with the standard. additionally I have created a way to dynamically register tool handlers: 

```csharp
MCPServer.AddToolHandler( new Tool() 
{
    Name = "sendMessage",
    Description = "craft a message to send to a recipient",
    InputSchema = new InputSchema {
        Type = "object",
        Required = ["input"],
        Properties = new Dictionary<string, ParameterSchema>{
            {"name",    new ParameterSchema{Type="string", Description="the name of the recipient"}},
            {"message", new ParameterSchema{Type="string", Description="the message to send to the recipient"}}
        }
    }
}, (string name, string message) => { return $"hello, {name}.\n{message}"; });
```

Does this align with your use case?
